{title: When I Survey the Wondrous Cross}
{artist: Isaac Watts}
{key: G}
{tempo: 66}
{time: 4/4}
{meta: tune HAMBURG - Lowell Mason (1824)}
{meta: public_domain Text 1707, tune 1824: both published before 1931 (US public domain); all authors, translators and composers died over 70 years ago. Chords and section layout: original simple arrangement by Tabernacle Tools. Original verses only, no modern additions.}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
When [G]I sur[C]vey the [G]wondrous [D]cross
On [G]which the [C]Prince of [D]glory [G]died,
My [G]richest [C]gain I [G]count but [Em]loss,
And [C]pour con[G]tempt on [D]all my [G]pride.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
For[G]bid it, [C]Lord, that [G]I should [D]boast,
Save [G]in the [C]death of [D]Christ my [G]God!
All [G]the vain [C]things that [G]charm me [Em]most,
I [C]sacri[G]fice them [D]to His [G]blood.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
See, [G]from His [C]head, His [G]hands, His [D]feet,
Sor[G]row and [C]love flow [D]mingled [G]down!
Did [G]e'er such [C]love and [G]sorrow [Em]meet,
Or [C]thorns com[G]pose so [D]rich a [G]crown?
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Were [G]the whole [C]realm of [G]nature [D]mine,
That [G]were a [C]present [D]far too [G]small;
Love [G]so a[C]mazing, [G]so di[Em]vine,
De[C]mands my [G]soul, my [D]life, my [G]all.
{end_of_verse}
